WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.485
Several RAM-backed Linux patterns appear in real systems. The first is live media copied into RAM.

2
00:00:06.485 --> 00:00:11.017
A live distribution often starts from a compressed read-only image.

3
00:00:11.017 --> 00:00:16.720
With the right distribution support, that image may be copied into memory during boot.

4
00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.955
After loading, the system can run with fewer reads from the original boot media.

5
00:00:21.955 --> 00:00:31.331
This is useful for portable environments, recovery sessions, and machines where the boot device should be removed or used less after startup.

6
00:00:31.331 --> 00:00:40.082
A second pattern uses a read-only base image with a writable RAM-backed overlay. The lower layer contains the base operating system.

7
00:00:40.082 --> 00:00:49.146
The upper layer records changes. A merged view looks writable to the running system. If the upper layer is tmpfs, changes vanish at reboot.

8
00:00:49.146 --> 00:00:56.178
This is common in live systems and can also be useful in kiosks, labs, and systems that should reset cleanly.

9
00:00:56.178 --> 00:00:59.615
A third pattern makes only selected paths volatile.

10
00:00:59.615 --> 00:01:12.117
For example, temporary caches, selected logs, browser caches, package download caches, or scratch directories may live in tmpfs while the rest of the installed system remains persistent.

11
00:01:12.117 --> 00:01:20.164
This is less dramatic than a full RAM root, but often easier to support and recover. A fourth pattern is a full volatile root.

12
00:01:20.164 --> 00:01:29.931
This is more aggressive. The whole working root may reset every boot, with persistence only through deliberately mounted paths or save mechanisms.

13
00:01:29.931 --> 00:01:37.666
It can suit training machines, clean-room workflows, and controlled appliances, but it requires careful planning.

14
00:01:37.666 --> 00:01:40.244
A fifth pattern is hybrid persistence.

15
00:01:40.244 --> 00:01:50.870
The base may be read-only, the running changes may be temporary, but /home, selected configuration, machine identity, logs, or explicit snapshots may persist.

16
00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:58.840
Hybrid systems usually match real operational needs better than pure volatility. The right pattern depends on the goal.

17
00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:10.638
Reducing disk writes, creating a disposable session, improving read-heavy boot media behavior, protecting a base image, and supporting recovery all lead to different designs.

18
00:02:10.638 --> 00:02:15.717
Start with the use case, not with a command copied from another distribution.
